## Finding

The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says “one issue, one fix queued”.
The component logic and tenant directory show two causes. Keep separate engineering
tickets for the shared parser and the booking form hint; fixing either leaves the other.
This review covers the export dated 4 October 2026. It does not establish the current
production state or prove that a queued fix has shipped.

<IncidentReview />

## Shared parser

[Engineering ticket](https://github.com/pykSpyro/megaDev_challenge3/issues/1)

The shared parser reads dates month first even when the tenant uses day-first dates.
For example, report R-41200 from Harbour Dental describes 7 April becoming 4 July.
Use the tenant's locale, not the reporter's browser locale, to establish the expected order.
R-41205 belongs here even though intake tagged it `hint`: Morrow Tax uses `fr-FR`
and booking-v2, and the report came through the app.

Engineering should make parsing match the tenant's date convention, reject invalid dates,
and test ambiguous dates through both app and email paths. Verify the final invite as well
as the parsed value. The export identifies the behavior, not the parser implementation.

## Booking-v3 hint

[Engineering ticket](https://github.com/pykSpyro/megaDev_challenge3/issues/2)

Booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` for day-first tenants, while the form reads input in the
tenant's order. People who follow the hint can receive the wrong date. This is a separate
UI and input-contract defect. Report R-41230 is one example: Copper Studio uses `es-ES`
and booking-v3, the report came through the form, and intake incorrectly tagged it `parser`.

Engineering should derive the hint and parsing from the same tenant date convention.
Test Alder Physio (`de-DE`), Copper Studio (`es-ES`), and Pale Harbour (`pt-PT`), with
browser locales that differ from the tenant. Check that day-first booking-v2 forms and
non-form paths remain correctly handled. Preserve the user's intended date in the invite.

## What the timeline shows

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports began in April; a large merge batch happened in July; reports continued into
October. A July merge timestamp records desk activity, not the start of the fault.
“Known” and “queued” therefore do not mean resolved. Track first receipt, merge time,
fix deployment and verified recovery separately.

## How the desk should triage the next report

The [current triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) and its
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) treat matching normalized
subjects as duplicates. The executable examples disagree: identical subjects can have
different causes, and different subjects can share a cause.

Use subject matches to find candidates, then read the report and check tenant locale,
form version, channel, actual input and observed output. Merge only when the cause matches.
Unknown tenants stay unplaced until investigated; missing evidence is not a reason to merge.
Browser locale and intake tags are clues, not the grouping rule. Deduplicate overlapping
export pages by report ID before counting.

Keep accepted decision 0001 intact as history. Propose a new decision that supersedes it
with this cause-based rule, and update the live triage procedure when that decision is accepted.
Recheck the live desk before relying on this exported review: new reports and fixes may
have arrived since export. Reproduce each failure, assign owners to both tickets, and close
them independently only after the respective fix is verified.

